1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:05,452
Most people reading a test result reach for one of two conclusions: this worked, or

2
00:00:05,452 --> 00:00:06,190
this didn't.

3
00:00:06,190 --> 00:00:09,079
That's an honest instinct, and it's incomplete.

4
00:00:09,079 --> 00:00:13,874
A huge share of real results belong in a third bucket neither of those options

5
00:00:13,874 --> 00:00:14,305
covers.

6
00:00:14,305 --> 00:00:17,194
Think about why a binary read feels so natural.

7
00:00:17,194 --> 00:00:22,235
Everything up to this point in the course rewarded a clean decision: kill or keep,

8
00:00:22,235 --> 00:00:23,095
ship or don't.

9
00:00:23,095 --> 00:00:24,817
A results read is different.

10
00:00:24,817 --> 00:00:28,136
The most common honest answer isn't a decision at all.

11
00:00:28,136 --> 00:00:32,931
It's a deferral, and deferrals don't feel like progress, even when they're the

12
00:00:32,931 --> 00:00:33,730
correct call.

13
00:00:33,730 --> 00:00:38,710
Noise means exactly what Section 7.4 already taught you to look for: a result too

14
00:00:38,710 --> 00:00:42,644
small, too fragile, or too recent to actually mean anything yet.

15
00:00:42,644 --> 00:00:47,377
Sorting into winner or loser when the honest answer is noise isn't a harmless

16
00:00:47,377 --> 00:00:48,300
simplification.

17
00:00:48,300 --> 00:00:53,156
It's the single most common way a testing programme quietly stops being honest.

18
00:00:53,156 --> 00:00:55,062
Why does this happen so easily?

19
00:00:55,062 --> 00:00:59,918
Because "still noise" is an unsatisfying thing to report, especially after real

20
00:00:59,918 --> 00:01:02,008
budget's already gone into a test.

21
00:01:02,008 --> 00:01:04,529
Winner and loser both feel like progress.

22
00:01:04,529 --> 00:01:09,262
Noise feels like nothing happened, even though correctly identifying noise is

23
00:01:09,262 --> 00:01:13,319
exactly the discipline Section 7.4 spent a whole lecture building.

24
00:01:13,869 --> 00:01:18,804
Run these three questions in order, and notice the tree only produces a winner

25
00:01:18,804 --> 00:01:23,928
through one specific path: real margin past the kill rule, a genuine gap past the

26
00:01:23,928 --> 00:01:28,040
CAC ceiling, and a sample robust enough to survive the swap test.

27
00:01:28,040 --> 00:01:32,658
Miss any one of those and the honest label is noise or loser, not winner.

28
00:01:32,658 --> 00:01:37,719
This is deliberately harder to reach than just "beat the ceiling once." A single

29
00:01:37,719 --> 00:01:43,033
result that clears the CAC line on a tiny sample still routes back to noise, because

30
00:01:43,033 --> 00:01:48,221
Section 7.4 already showed you how easily a small sample produces a gap that isn't

31
00:01:48,221 --> 00:01:48,537
real.

32
00:01:48,537 --> 00:01:53,725
Notice, too, that a loser can arrive two different ways on this tree, and both are

33
00:01:53,725 --> 00:01:54,737
genuinely final.

34
00:01:54,737 --> 00:01:57,900
Failing the kill rule outright is the obvious one.

35
00:01:57,900 --> 00:02:02,834
Passing the kill rule but never producing a swap-proof gap past the ceiling is

36
00:02:02,834 --> 00:02:07,895
quieter, and easy to miss if you stop checking once an ad set survives its first

37
00:02:07,895 --> 00:02:08,528
threshold.

38
00:02:08,528 --> 00:02:13,715
Walk through what happens to an ad set that never quite clears row two, week after

39
00:02:13,715 --> 00:02:14,032
week.

40
00:02:14,032 --> 00:02:17,258
It's not failing outright, so it never gets killed.

41
00:02:17,258 --> 00:02:19,346
It's also never actually winning.

42
00:02:19,346 --> 00:02:24,660
Left unchecked, that ad set can sit in your account indefinitely, spending steadily,

43
00:02:24,660 --> 00:02:29,658
contributing nothing, simply because nobody ran the third question against it a

44
00:02:29,658 --> 00:02:30,417
second time.

45
00:02:30,967 --> 00:02:35,248
Here's a real result, read against the tree instead of against a feeling.

46
00:02:35,248 --> 00:02:40,116
Two point six percent click-through, nearly three times the old gut-era baseline of

47
00:02:40,116 --> 00:02:41,523
zero point nine percent.

48
00:02:41,523 --> 00:02:46,215
Nineteen dollars CAC, comfortably under the twenty-four-dollar ceiling, not just

49
00:02:46,215 --> 00:02:47,212
scraping past it.

50
00:02:47,212 --> 00:02:51,962
Notice what made this a genuine winner rather than a lucky early lead: it cleared

51
00:02:51,962 --> 00:02:55,247
every question on the tree, not just the flattering one.

52
00:02:55,247 --> 00:03:00,173
Against six other ad sets that were mostly noise, this is the one result that earned

53
00:03:00,173 --> 00:03:02,519
its verdict instead of being handed one.

54
00:03:02,519 --> 00:03:07,211
Would this still count as a winner if the CAC had landed at twenty-three dollars

55
00:03:07,211 --> 00:03:11,844
instead of nineteen, barely under the ceiling rather than comfortably under it?

56
00:03:11,844 --> 00:03:16,653
Worth asking yourself that question now, because a margin that thin is exactly the

57
00:03:16,653 --> 00:03:20,817
kind Section 7.4's swap test exists to interrogate before you trust it.

58
00:03:21,367 --> 00:03:25,541
One honest expectation before you run this tree on your own results.

59
00:03:25,541 --> 00:03:30,576
Out of Harlan's first batch of ad sets, most of them landed in noise or loser, and

60
00:03:30,576 --> 00:03:32,970
only one earned a clean winner verdict.

61
00:03:32,970 --> 00:03:36,101
That ratio isn't a sign the testing machine failed.

62
00:03:36,101 --> 00:03:39,846
It's what an honest read of a real batch actually looks like.

63
00:03:39,846 --> 00:03:44,880
A tree that called every ad set a winner or a loser would be lying to you far more

64
00:03:44,880 --> 00:03:46,415
often than this one does.

65
00:03:46,415 --> 00:03:51,449
Hold that ratio against Section 1.4's volume maths, because it's the same argument

66
00:03:51,449 --> 00:03:53,905
showing up again from a different angle.

67
00:03:53,905 --> 00:03:59,062
You generate a real batch, not one hero ad, precisely because most individual angles

68
00:03:59,062 --> 00:04:00,781
won't earn a winner verdict.

69
00:04:00,781 --> 00:04:04,341
The system was never designed to make every angle succeed.

70
00:04:04,341 --> 00:04:09,130
It was designed to find the one that does, honestly, among several that don't.

71
00:04:09,130 --> 00:04:12,322
That reframing matters for morale as much as method.

72
00:04:12,322 --> 00:04:16,988
If you expect most of a batch to win, a normal result feels like a string of

73
00:04:16,988 --> 00:04:17,541
failures.

74
00:04:17,541 --> 00:04:22,636
If you expect most of a batch to land in noise or loser by design, that same result

75
00:04:22,636 --> 00:04:26,443
feels like exactly what a real test looks like, because it is.

76
00:04:26,993 --> 00:04:31,529
Next lecture picks up exactly where this one ends: what happens to a real winner,

77
00:04:31,529 --> 00:04:36,121
weeks later, when the same creative that earned this verdict quietly stops earning

78
00:04:36,121 --> 00:04:36,289
it.

79
00:04:36,289 --> 00:04:40,097
Before that, run this tree on whatever test you have live right now.

80
00:04:40,097 --> 00:04:44,689
Answer the three questions honestly, in order, and write down the verdict, even if

81
00:04:44,689 --> 00:04:45,305
it's noise.

82
00:04:45,305 --> 00:04:46,705
Especially if it's noise.

83
00:04:46,705 --> 00:04:51,241
That's the result most people skip past, and it's the one this lecture most wants

84
00:04:51,241 --> 00:04:52,977
you to be able to name plainly.
